[2026-08-30T14:34:43.369Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=d2129f9f8516c1237b3da462cdf66d4d] Topic: Stripped Next.js starter boilerplate and set homepage to "ghost AI"
[2026-08-30T14:34:43.369Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=d2129f9f8516c1237b3da462cdf66d4d]   Trigger:   The generated Next.js starter still contained default boilerplate — sample homepage content, unused theme CSS, and placeholder icon assets — that needed to be cleared out before the app could carry its own identity.
[2026-08-30T14:34:43.369Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=d2129f9f8516c1237b3da462cdf66d4d]   Response:  - Reduced `app/globals.css` down to just the Tailwind import, removing the default light/dark CSS custom-property theme and body styling that shipped with the starter.
- Deleted the starter SVG icons in `public/` (`file.svg`, `globe.svg`, `next.svg`, `vercel.svg`, `window.svg`) while explicitly keeping `favicon.ico` in place.
- Replaced the default Next.js starter markup in `app/page.tsx` with a minimal component rendering a centered "ghost AI" div; confirmed no `page.module.css` existed so there was nothing to remove there.
[2026-08-30T14:34:43.369Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=d2129f9f8516c1237b3da462cdf66d4d]   Decisions: The favicon was kept while every other default icon asset was removed, preserving the browser tab identity while clearing out unused starter branding.
[2026-08-30T14:34:43.369Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=d2129f9f8516c1237b3da462cdf66d4d] Topic: Authored product, architecture, UI, and workflow specification docs for Ghost AI
[2026-08-30T14:34:43.369Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=d2129f9f8516c1237b3da462cdf66d4d]   Trigger:   The project needed a foundational specification before feature work could begin — defining what Ghost AI is, how it should be built technically, and what rules an AI agent should follow while implementing it.
[2026-08-30T14:34:43.369Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=d2129f9f8516c1237b3da462cdf66d4d]   Response:  - Added `context/project-overview.md` describing Ghost AI as a real-time collaborative system-design workspace, including its core user flow, feature set, in/out-of-scope items, and success criteria; updated `AGENTS.md` to require reading all context files in a defined order before implementation and to keep `context/progress-tracker.md` current.
- Added `context/architecture-context.md` defining the stack (Next.js, Clerk, Prisma/PostgreSQL, Liveblocks/React Flow, Trigger.dev, Vercel Blob), system boundaries between API routes, background tasks, and shared libraries, the storage split between database and blob storage, and a set of invariants.
- Added `context/ui-context.md` and `context/code-standards.md` documenting a dark-only visual theme with a full color token table, typography, canvas node/edge styling conventions, and implementation rules (strict TypeScript, server-components-by-default, thin API routes, no raw color classes).
[2026-08-30T14:34:43.369Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=d2129f9f8516c1237b3da462cdf66d4d]   Decisions: - **Split storage by artifact size**: project metadata, ownership, and task run records live in a relational database, while large generated artifacts like canvas snapshots and specs are stored in blob storage and referenced by URL — keeping the database lean while still supporting large generated content.
- **Background workers own long-running AI work**: system-design generation and spec generation run as durable background tasks rather than inside request handlers, so API routes stay fast and generation can survive interruptions.
- **Spec-driven build process**: development guidance requires reading the product, architecture, UI, and standards docs before writing code and keeping a progress tracker in sync with actual implementation state, giving future work (human or AI-driven) a single source of truth instead of ad hoc interpretation.
